[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"TensorFlow Serving Architecture and Configuration Tutorial"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"TensorFlow Serving is a flexible, high-performance serving system for machine learning models, designed for production environments. It deals with the life cycle of one or more models, receiving requests over gRPC or REST, and executing inference without requiring custom infrastructure."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Core Architecture Overview"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"The TensorFlow Serving architecture is built around several key abstractions that manage how models are loaded, versioned, and served:"},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Servables: The underlying objects that compute inferences, such as a TensorFlow SavedModel graph session."},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Managers: Components that manage the full lifecycle of Servables, including loading, unloading, and reloading."},{"order":7,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Sources: Modules that find and provide Servables by creating Loader instances, typically reading from a file system."},{"order":8,"type":"bullet_point","text":"loaders: The mechanism that encapsulates the process of loading a Servable from storage into memory."},{"order":9,"type":"subheading","text":"Model Directory Structure"},{"order":10,"type":"paragraph","text":"To serve models efficiently, TensorFlow Serving expects a specific directory structure where the model name is the parent directory, and version numbers (represented as integers) are subdirectories containing the SavedModel assets."},{"order":11,"type":"code","text":"/models/
  my_model/
    1610000000/
      saved_model.pb
      variables/
        variables.data-00000-of-00001
        variables.index
    1610000001/
      saved_model.pb
      variables/
        variables.data-00000-of-00001
        variables.index"},{"order":12,"type":"subheading","text":"Advanced Configuration via Model Config File"},{"order":13,"type":"paragraph","text":"While you can launch TensorFlow Serving with a single model using command-line flags, production environments usually require serving multiple models or fine-tuning version policies. This is accomplished using a model config file (model_config_list)."},{"order":14,"type":"code","text":"model_config_list: {
  config: {
    name: "fraud_detector",
    base_path: "/models/fraud_detector",
    model_platform: "tensorflow",
    model_version_policy: {
      latest: {
        num_versions: 2
      }
    }
  },
  config: {
    name: "recommendation_engine",
    base_path: "/models/recommend_eng",
    model_platform: "tensorflow",
    model_version_policy: {
      specific: {
        versions: [1, 2]
      }
    }
  }
}"},{"order":15,"type":"subheading","text":"Launching TensorFlow Serving"},{"order":16,"type":"paragraph","text":"Using Docker is the standard approach to run TensorFlow Serving. You can mount your local model directory and point the container to your configuration file."},{"order":17,"type":"code","text":"docker run -p 8501:8501 \
  --mount type=bind,source=/path/to/models,target=/models \
  --mount type=bind,source=/path/to/models.config,target=/models/models.config \
  tensorflow/serving \
  --model_config_file=/models/models.config \
  --model_config_file_poll_wait_seconds=60"},{"order":18,"type":"subheading","text":"Sending Inference Requests"},{"order":19,"type":"paragraph","text":"Once TensorFlow Serving is running, you can send HTTP POST requests to the REST API endpoint corresponding to your model name and version."},{"order":20,"type":"code","text":"curl -d '{"instances": [[1.5, 2.3, 5.1, 8.9]]}' \
  -X POST http://localhost:8501/v1/models/fraud_detector:predict"}]
